# AAA Life company guardrails

These are company-level constraints, not project preferences. They outrank convenience, deadlines,
and my own suggestions. If a request conflicts with one of these, say so plainly rather than quietly
complying.

Sources: AAA Life Insurance Company Corporate Compliance Policy (CCP), the AAA Life Brand Visual
Style Guide (MAR 2025), and WCAG 2.2. The CCP and style guide are **proprietary and internal-use
only** - apply their frameworks, never reproduce or export their text.

## 1. Compliance escalation

- Compliance is the **second line of defense**. The design and marketing team owns day-to-day
  adherence; Compliance is not a safety net that makes carelessness fine.
- Any compliance **exception requires CCO approval**. A tight deadline is not an exception.
- Interpretation questions go to the **Compliance Department**. Legal risk goes to **Legal**.
- Suspected violations are reportable through management, Compliance, HR, or the **Speak-Up
  Helpline**, with non-retaliation protection. Surface this path if a request appears to knowingly
  skirt a rule.
- When work has real regulatory stakes, say so and recommend routing to Compliance. Never present a
  general best-practice baseline as a substitute for actual CCP procedure.

## 2. Advertising baseline

Apply to every piece of copy this project generates or drafts:

- Truthful and not misleading. No deceptive comparisons.
- **No guaranteed coverage or rates without qualification.** Flag any copy implying either.
- No implied government affiliation or endorsement.
- Testimonials must be genuine, current, and representative, with FTC endorsement disclosure.
- "No medical exam" claims must be accurate and qualified.
- Senior-targeted messaging must avoid scare tactics, false urgency, and overstated simplicity.
- Producer and agent CTAs must reflect valid state licensing language.
- Design patterns that pressure an unsuitable purchase - dark patterns, forced urgency, misleading
  visual hierarchy, hidden terms - are a **market conduct** issue, not a UX nitpick.

## 3. Disclaimer presets

Starting points for a brief's mandatories, drawn from the style guide's own samples. **Compliance
owns final wording** - these are drafts, never authorities:

- Underwritten by AAA Life Insurance Company, Livonia, MI.
- Products and features may vary by state.
- AAA Life is licensed in all states except New York.
- California Certificate of Authority #07861.
- See policy for full terms, conditions, and exclusions.
- Subject to underwriting; coverage is not in effect until the policy is issued.

## 4. Brand rules

- Use the **digital** palette on screens, never the print palette. The print red `#ED1C2F` gives
  white text only 4.37:1 and fails AA; the digital red `#E31E2E` is 4.68:1.
- **Red and bronze are never primary.** Red is reserved for a final submit action and genuine errors.
  The guide specifically cautions against red buttons on screens containing form fields, since red
  reads as an error state.
- Keep layouts light and cool.
- **Underline only clickable links.** Keep links bold, in blue or red.
- **Never pair Gotham with Open Sans.** Gotham is licensed and cannot ship in a web app, so Open Sans
  is used alone. Nunito Sans, Oswald, Roboto, and Gotham Condensed are not brand fonts.
- **Never alter the logo** - no stretching, recoloring, rotating, disassembling, or rebuilding from
  parts. Honor its clear space, equal to half the red oval's height on all sides.
- Deviations from the guide are recorded as dated exceptions in `docs/decisions/decision-log.md`
  pending brand-team sign-off. An undocumented deviation is drift.

## 5. Data privacy

- Requester **name, email, and department are PII**.
- **No PII leaves the device without privacy review.** This project stores everything in
  `localStorage` and makes no network call; changing that is a privacy decision, not a refactor.
- Never commit customer data, credentials, tokens, or `.env` files.
- Webfonts, analytics, and CDN assets are **third-party requests that leak IP address and user
  agent**. Self-host instead. This is why Google Fonts was removed from `index.html`.
- On a hosted, shared-machine origin, PII persisting in `localStorage` is a real exposure. Provide a
  way for a user to clear their own data on the device.

## 6. Least privilege across teams

- Cross-team request data is **summary only**: ALAN number, title, requesting team, status, and match
  reason.
- **Never** a brief body, an attachment, or a requester's email in a match card or any cross-team
  listing.
- Widening that visibility is a decision to be logged, never a default.

## 7. Recommendations must be auditable

- Any surfaced match, duplicate call, or suggestion **states the signal behind it** - "shares
  ALAN-29541-624-00", "same deliverable type and deadline week". No unexplained scoring in a
  compliance-sensitive workflow. A recommendation a user cannot audit gets either ignored or blindly
  trusted, and both are worse than no recommendation.

- **An absence of results is also a recommendation, and carries the same burden.** A bare "no
  duplicates found" reads as an organization-wide statement of fact. It must instead state the corpus
  it was drawn from and how complete that corpus was. A negative result is **never** presented as
  organization-wide unless the corpus actually was organization-wide.

- **Degrade coverage claims explicitly.** If a remote corpus is unreachable and the search silently
  falls back to local data while still implying full coverage, that is worse than returning nothing.
  Say what was actually searched and why it was incomplete.

## 8. Third-party vendors

Any new API, MCP server, LLM provider, analytics tool, font host, hosted service, or dependency
installed from outside the public npm registry is a **Vendor Management Policy consideration** and is
flagged before adoption, not after.

- Integrations start as **typed adapter stubs** with no live call.
- No live third-party API call without explicit approval.
- Credentials are a Vendor Management and privacy gate.
- **Never** put a secret in a `VITE_`-prefixed variable. Vite compiles those into the public client
  bundle. Server-side secrets belong behind a proxy.
- When resolving a dependency advisory, verify the replacement is legitimate. Convenience forks
  promising a quick fix are a known malware vector; `xlsx-enhanced` is a documented example.

## 9. Accessibility as compliance

- **WCAG 2.2 AA is the floor**, not the goal. ADA Title III exposure applies to insurer properties.
- Every recommendation carries a **Law-of-UX rationale and a WCAG citation**.
- **Contrast is computed, never asserted.** Use
  `.cursor/skills/verify-live-ux/scripts/contrast.py` and label every figure verified or estimated -
  including when a brand document itself makes an ADA claim. The style guide's own ADA guidance has
  been measured and found wrong in three places; see `docs/knowledge/brand-system.md`.
- An approved brand exception **never** approves a WCAG failure. Darken the value instead.
- Never convey state by color alone. Never rely on placeholder text as a label.

## 10. Copy corrections (standing rule)

- Spelling, grammar, and capitalization errors in any source are **corrected in shipped copy** rather
  than reproduced. Mocks are layout references, not copy decks.
- Known corrections: `Misson` to **Mission**; `que` to **queue**.
- Brand spellings: **Workfront**, **Monday.com**, **AAA Life Insurance Company**.
- Anything that changes **meaning** is surfaced to the user, not silently applied.
